---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout title="What we cover - Harbor Legal Independent Index" description="Every US state legislature, Congress, all 13 federal appeals circuits, and the Supreme Court. Nothing below appeals court level." activeNav="method">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">What we cover</p>
      <h1>Every legislature. Every appeals court. Nothing below.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Harbor Legal Independent Index draws a deliberate line. All 50 state legislatures and
        Congress on the bills side. All 13 federal appeals circuits and the Supreme Court on the
        courts side. Local courts and trial-level decisions are outside scope — they don't reshape
        the legal landscape the way appellate rulings do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Legislation</div>
          <div class="card-value">All 50 states + Congress</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Bills are collected daily from each state's official legislative system and from
            Congress.gov. Every bill is tracked from introduction through committee, chamber
            passage, and signing. States with active sessions update more frequently; off-session
            states are checked for interim activity.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Courts</div>
          <div class="card-value">Circuits and SCOTUS only</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Court coverage is limited to federal appeals circuits (CA1–CA11, DC Circuit, Federal
            Circuit) and the Supreme Court — including opinions, orders, cert grants, and cert
            denials. These are the decisions that change how laws are read and applied nationwide
            or across a region.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Classification</div>
          <div class="card-value">Areas of law</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Every bill and opinion is mapped to a plain-language legal domain — housing, labor,
            privacy, firearms, and others — so you can follow a topic across every jurisdiction
            without knowing the statutory codes or case names involved.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Freshness</div>
          <div class="card-value">Daily, where sessions allow</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Most states update the index daily. Some legislatures publish updates less frequently
            depending on session schedule and how their official systems expose data. The index
            reflects the most recent available state for each jurisdiction.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="surface">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we don't claim</p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 0.6rem">Being clear about the limits</h2>
        <div class="signal-stack">
          <div class="signal-item">
            Bill counts can include re-introductions from prior sessions; reports note this where it affects interpretation.
          </div>
          <div class="signal-item">
            Court coverage is intentionally narrow — trial courts and administrative bodies are outside scope, even when their decisions are significant locally.
          </div>
          <div class="signal-item">
            Some state data lags by a day or more depending on when official systems publish updates. Session-specific timing is noted per jurisdiction.
          </div>
          <div class="signal-item">
            The index describes what has happened — bills introduced, rulings issued, laws signed. It does not predict outcomes or provide legal advice.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
